<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig Boltzmann</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ludwig Eduard Boltzmann</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludwig Boltzmann was an Austrian mathematician and theoretical physicist whose chief achievements were the development of statistical mechanics and the statistical explanation of the second law of thermodynamics. Born in Erdberg, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, he studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> under Josef Stefan and taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and Vienna. In 1877 he gave the current definition of entropy as a measure of the statistical disorder of a system, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a> named the constant in it the Boltzmann constant. In his last years he defended his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-theory">atomic theories</a> against critics such as Ernst Mach, and he died by suicide at Duino, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, in 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-boltzmann.jpg" alt="Ludwig Boltzmann" width="330" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boltzmann-Ludwig.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Clement Greenberg placed it in mid-19th-century France, with Charles Baudelaire in literature and Manet in painting; William Everdell dated it to the 1870s, with Richard Dedekind’s mathematics and <b>Ludwig Boltzmann</b>’s statistical thermodynamics, and in painting to Georges Seurat’s Divisionism of 1885–1886.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
